※ pl4g-test: compile=fail
※ pl4g-test: expect-diag 4240 LANG_NUMBER_NOT_AN_INTEGER
※ pl4g-test: expect-diag 4240 LANG_NUMBER_NOT_AN_INTEGER
※ pl4g-test: expect-diag 4241 LANG_NUMBER_NOT_A_FLOAT

※ `⎕float` makes a floating-point number out of a whole one.  A floating-point
※ number becomes another by widening, which needs no name, and a truth value is
※ no number at all.  The type it makes is a floating-point type, and nothing
※ else: a whole number made out of a whole number is a widening or a narrowing.

let std := ⎕import("std")

let x: f32 = 1.5f32
let yes: bool = true

@[startup]
fn main(init: std.Init) → u6:
    let a: f64 = ⎕float(x, ⌜f64⌝)
    let b: f64 = ⎕float(yes, ⌜f64⌝)
    let c: u64 = ⎕float(7u8, ⌜u64⌝)
    0u6
